That sounds like you're in some big hurry to leave
the foot of the stairs!

I find that standing on the downstairs, letting my
pet or pets kill monsters on each Mines level until
the monsters stop coming, gives me the chance to run
back up, preferably with my pet, and recover
hitpoints before going back down. One issue is that
monsters have  stupid navigation algorithm, like
pets, and can get trapped in cul-de-sacs where they
just accumulate their numbers, then overwhelm you
when you walk past the entrance.

If instead, you just stay put at the foot of the
stairs until dwarves or rock moles connect all the
cul-de-sacs to where you are, your pet will
eventually leave the level nearly free of monsters,
at which point it is safe for you to go exploring.
With some luck, many of the traps will now be marked
with monster death-dropped goodies, making
exploration a bit safer.

Standing on the stairs for a long time, besides
letting your PC duck upstairs when wounded by
"missiles from the dark", also solves one other
problem, that of the pet too busy eating to fight.
If Puss.In.Boots is too busy eating to fight the
next monster, duck upstairs, rest five turns, go
back down, and very likely Puss will be gnashing
the next oncoming monster. This is one of the places
where having time as in "motion" freeze when the PC
is off-level, while time as in "healing/eating"
keeps running, is really abusable.

Too many times I see the protection racket described
as going to the Mines Town priest(ess) "in haste".

The only benefit to hurrying is a food benefit, but
if you've solved your food problem another way than
by eating corpses ravenously, you are better advised
to take a lot of time, without killing a lot of
stuff. So long as you don't level up, the RNG
doesn't send harder and harder monsters, it sends,
relative to the strength of your pet, easier and
easier monsters, and they are also relatively easier
over time with respect to your incrementally
improving armor class.

